# Ooda CLI — Dispatch Pattern

File: cli/docs/dispatch.oot

The ooda CLI is a 30-line argv router in `cli/main.oo`. The full dispatch table is a cascade of `if sub == "<name>"` branches followed by the 3 spawn entries. There is no dynamic plugin loader; the dispatch is hard-coded and readable.

## The mental model

```
user types:  ooda opm add foo
                │
                ▼
        cli/main.oo argv router
                │
                ▼
        spawn("opm", ["add", "foo"])  ← subprocess, stdio inherited
                │
                ▼
        /opm/ binary runs, does its work, exits
                │
                ▼
        ooda exits with the same code
```

The same pattern applies to every subcommand, in-process or spawned. The router is the only place that decides which handler runs. Every handler trusts that the router already validated the subcommand name.

## The dispatch shape

```oo
pub fn main(...args: List[String]) -> Result<Int, String> {
    if list_len(args) < 1 { print_help(); return Err("missing subcommand"); }
    let sub: String = list_get(args, 0);
    let rest: List[String] = list_slice(args, 1, list_len(args));

    // build cluster — most return void; we wrap to Ok(0)
    if sub == "build"   { run_oodac_build(...);  return Ok(0); }
    if sub == "run"     { run_oodac_run(...);    return Ok(0); }
    if sub == "gen"     { run_ooda_gen(...);     return Ok(0); }
    ...
    if sub == "fmt"     { return Ok(run_ooda_fmt(...)); }

    // token cluster — 12 sub-subcommands
    if sub == "token"   { run_ooda_token(...);   return Ok(0); }

    // external packages — spawn, do not link
    if sub == "opm"     { return spawn("opm", rest); }
    if sub == "lsp"     { return spawn("lsp", rest); }
    if sub == "mcp"     { return spawn("mcp", rest); }

    // unknown
    print_help();
    return Err("ooda: unknown subcommand: " + sub);
}
```

The cascade is `if`-not-`match` because some branches (init) want to inspect `rest[0]` before falling through. A pure `match` would force every branch to extract the same args, which clutters the read.

## The lifecycle: parse, validate, execute, report

Every ooda invocation passes through the same 4 stages, regardless of subcommand.

### 1. Parse

`main.oo` reads `argv[0]` as the subcommand and slices `argv[1..]` as the per-command tail. The slice is the boundary between the router and the handler. Handlers never see `argv[0]`.

### 2. Validate

Each handler validates its own argv. The validation includes: path traversal checks, file existence checks under `FsReadCap`, flag set checks, and cap-aware checks (e.g. the install handler refuses any `--to` outside `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR`). Failures print `ERR\t<cluster>\t<msg>` and `process_exit(2)`.

### 3. Execute

The handler does its work. For in-process commands, this is plain in-memory ooda code. For spawned commands, `spawn()` in `main.oo` invokes `sys_exec_wait` with the resolved binary path. Stdio is inherited from the parent process.

### 4. Report

The handler returns. In-process handlers return `Result[Int, String]` or `void`; `main.oo` wraps a `void` return into `Ok(0)`. Spawned handlers return the subprocess's exit code via `Result`. The process exits with that code; the host shell or editor sees it directly.

## The cmd_* naming convention

Inside each subdir, the public entry point follows `run_ooda_<verb>` or `cli_<verb>_<noun>`. The prefix is the file's domain.

| Prefix | Files | Examples |
|--------|-------|----------|
| `run_ooda_<verb>` | every per-command file | `run_oodac_build`, `run_ooda_fix`, `run_ooda_token` |
| `cli_<verb>_<noun>` | init, args | `cli_init_project`, `cli_run_args` |
| `cmd_<noun>` | token sub-subcommand files | `cmd_token_status`, `cmd_token_report` |
| `find_` / `refuse_` / `require_` | helper entry points | `find_oodac`, `refuse_cli`, `require_writedir` |

The `cmd_*` pattern in `token/` is the closest the cli/ tree comes to a generic dispatcher: `cmd_token.oo` reads `args[1]`, picks one of 12 names, and calls the matching `cmd_*.oo` function. The names are stable; the dispatch is a string compare.

## Why subprocess for opm, lsp, mcp?

| Question | Answer |
|----------|--------|
| Why subprocess? | Version decoupling. opm, lsp, mcp each have their own VERSION. No lockstep. |
| Why stdio-inherited? | Editors connect their stdio to ooda; lsp/mcp need that stdio. Subprocess stdio is inherited by default. |
| Why is ooda the meta-router? | One command, many packages. No need to remember `opm`, `lsp`, `mcp`, `ooda build` separately. |
| Why no library link? | Linking creates a hard version dep. opm 0.8 would force ooda to re-release. |

## What goes into cli/ (in-process)

- Build, deliver, introspect: build, run, gen, test, install, bench, init, context, health
- Text operations: fix, digest
- Formatter: fmt
- Token subsystem: token (12 sub-subcommands)

These all run in-process because they do not have their own packages. The ooda binary is the only home for them.

## What gets spawned (external packages)

- `opm` (from /opm/ repo)
- `lsp` (from /lsp/ repo)
- `mcp` (from /mcp/ repo)

These all run as subprocesses. ooda does not link them.

## Failure paths

| Failure | Source | Exit code | Output |
|---------|--------|-----------|--------|
| missing subcommand | `main.oo` | 2 | help text + `Err("ooda: missing subcommand")` |
| unknown subcommand | `main.oo` | 2 | help text + `Err("ooda: unknown subcommand: <name>")` |
| missing file arg | per-command | 2 | `ERR\t<cluster>\t<msg>` |
| unreadable file | per-command | 2 | `ERR\t<cluster>\tunreadable file: <path>` |
| traversal / unsafe path | per-command | 1 or 2 | `ERR\t<cluster>\trejected traversal: <path>` |
| cap denied | runtime | 1 | `E_CAP: ...` |
| subprocess failure | spawn | 1 | whatever the subprocess printed |
